# Building one module

`LoomCorpusAstBuilder` compiles one `.loom` file. It runs the file through every pass in turn: it reads the file's bytes, parses them into a tree, runs the row of small passes over the tree, and builds the product. It returns a `LoomModule`: the document beside its `product`, the author's code with each anchor resolved to an edge.

It builds that one module and no more. It discovers no corpus and keeps no cache — `LoomCompiler` does both, listing the file's sibling looms and memoising each result in `LoomMemo`. A cross-file `::[…]` reference stays unresolved here as well. It is inlined later, at projection, by `fromProduct`.

The builder owns no files. It reads through a `Source` the caller hands it, so the same build runs over a real filesystem for the tangler, a Volar document host for the editor, or an in-memory fake for a test.

## The source it reads through

`Source` is what the builder reads through — a small interface the caller provides, never the filesystem directly. Its `read` method returns a file's text by its path, and fails with a `ReadError` when the file cannot be read. `LoomCompiler` supplies the real implementation, `DocumentSource`, so the builder reads through the caller rather than the filesystem.

`list` is the other half. Given a loom's path, it returns the paths of the looms that share that loom's corpus, the looms `LoomCompiler` builds together with it. Which looms those are depends on where the looms are kept, so the source decides, and the compiler never walks a folder. `list` is itself an `Option`: a caller that hands in a single document in memory carries `Option.none`, and the compiler then sees a corpus of one. The `DocumentSource` service the CLI and the editor use carries `Option.some` of a listing that asks the `WorkspaceSource` service, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes.

`ReadError` names a file the source cannot read — one that is missing, or one the operating system refuses to open — and carries the path and the underlying cause. Its message reads `Cannot read <path>: <cause>`. The build catches it and recovers, so a file that cannot be read becomes a diagnostic on that file rather than a crash.

=>

export interface Source {
  readonly read: (path: Location) => Effect.Effect<string, ReadError>
  readonly list: Option.Option<(loom: Location) => Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Location>>>
}

export class ReadError extends Data.TaggedError('ReadError')<{
  readonly path: Location
  readonly cause: unknown
}> {
  get message(): string {
    return `Cannot read ${this.path}: ${
      this.cause instanceof Error ? this.cause.message : String(this.cause)
    }`
  }
}

## The chain of passes

`build` runs one file through the whole chain, each pass consuming the one before it. The chain has two halves, and the builder gives each half as a method of its own, because the compiler sometimes runs them apart.

`parse` is the first half. It reads the file and parses it. `LoomSourceRanges` cuts the text into the range of every line, and fails when the file mixes line terminators. The three passes after it cannot fail, and each is a pure function over the whole list the pass before it gave. `WeftClassifier` turns the ranges into wefts, `WeftTokeniser` fills each weft's tokens, and `LoomAstBuilder` folds the wefts into a `ParsedDocument`. `parse` gives a `ParsedLoom`: the loom's path, the text it read, and that tree.

`finish` is the second half, and it takes a `ParsedLoom` the rest of the way. It hands the tree to `LoomDocumentBuilder`, which runs the row of small passes and returns the `LoomDocument`, as the chapter "Small passes over the tree" describes. It then runs `ProductBuilder` over the document to compose its `product`, and stops there. `build` is `parse` followed by `finish`. The passes are peers here — no pass hides another behind a single call. Three settings resolved for the file ride in from the caller and thread through the chain: the anchor delimiters `WeftTokeniser` cuts with, the primary language `ProductBuilder` falls back to, and the workspace variables it interpolates into a warp's value.

One more thing rides in beside the settings: `declared`, what the workspace's tropes declare, as the `Declared` of the chapter "Small passes over the tree". The row of small passes reads every pair of braces against it. They come from the book and not from the file, so the caller gathers them first, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes. A build that is handed `nothingDeclared` reads the file with core Loom alone.

Only `finish` reads `declared`, and that is why the chain is cut where it is. Nothing in `parse` depends on the book. So a caller that does not know yet what the tropes declare parses each loom, learns the declared kinds from what it parsed, and then finishes each loom once.

The chain is total: it never throws, and it always yields a module. Three failures can stop it before a real document exists. A file the source cannot read raises `ReadError`; a mixed line terminator the parse chain cannot pin to a node raises `MixedEOL`; and a delimiter pair the package configured badly raises one of the self-describing `InvalidAnchorDelims` errors. `parse` catches all three and recovers to an empty document carrying the error — `emptyDocument` with the read failure's message, `emptyDocumentFor` for a mixed terminator, and `emptyDocument` with the delimiter error's own `message`. So `finish` runs the small passes and the product pass over that document like any other. A file that cannot be read still yields a module, one whose health carries the read error. Every other malformation rides in the document's own health.

=>

export interface ParsedLoom {
  readonly path: Location
  readonly text: string
  readonly tree: ParsedDocument
}

export class LoomCorpusAstBuilder extends Context.Service<LoomCorpusAstBuilder>()(
  'LoomCorpusAstBuilder',
  {
    make: Effect.gen(function* () {
      const sourceRanges = yield* LoomSourceRanges
      const classify = yield* WeftClassifier
      const tokenise = yield* WeftTokeniser
      const astBuilder = yield* LoomAstBuilder
      const documents = yield* LoomDocumentBuilder
      const products = yield* ProductBuilder

      const parse = (
        source: Source,
        path: Location,
        delims: AnchorDelims = defaultAnchorDelims,
      ): Effect.Effect<ParsedLoom> =>
        source.read(path).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((text) => {
            const onBadDelims = (err: InvalidAnchorDelims) =>
              Effect.succeed(emptyDocument(text, err.message))
            return checkAnchorDelims(delims).pipe(
              Effect.andThen(sourceRanges.rangesOf(text)),
              Effect.map((ranges) =>
                pipe(
                  ranges,
                  classify.classifyWefts(text),
                  tokenise.tokeniseWefts(text, delims),
                  astBuilder.build,
                ),
              ),
              Effect.catchTag('MixedEOL', (err) => Effect.succeed(emptyDocumentFor(text, err))),
              Effect.catchTags({
                EmptyAnchorDelims: onBadDelims,
                IdenticalAnchorDelims: onBadDelims,
                WhitespaceAnchorDelims: onBadDelims,
                ReservedAnchorDelims: onBadDelims,
              }),
              Effect.map((tree): ParsedLoom => ({ path, text, tree })),
            )
          }),
          Effect.catchTag('ReadError', (err) =>
            Effect.succeed<ParsedLoom>({ path, text: '', tree: emptyDocument('', err.message) }),
          ),
        )

      const finish = (
        loom: ParsedLoom,
        primaryLanguage?: string,
        variables: Record<string, string> = {},
        declared: Declared = nothingDeclared,
      ): Effect.Effect<LoomModule> => {
        const doc = documents.build(loom.tree, declared)
        return products
          .build(doc, loom.path, primaryLanguage, variables)
          .pipe(Effect.map((product) => ({ path: loom.path, text: loom.text, doc, product })))
      }

      const build = (
        source: Source,
        path: Location,
        delims: AnchorDelims = defaultAnchorDelims,
        primaryLanguage?: string,
        variables: Record<string, string> = {},
        declared: Declared = nothingDeclared,
      ): Effect.Effect<LoomModule> =>
        parse(source, path, delims).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((loom) => finish(loom, primaryLanguage, variables, declared)),
        )

      return { parse, finish, build }
    }),
  },
) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make).pipe(
    Layer.provide(
      Layer.mergeAll(
        LoomSourceRanges.layer,
        WeftClassifier.layer,
        WeftTokeniser.layer,
        LoomAstBuilder.layer,
        LoomDocumentBuilder.layer,
        ProductBuilder.layer,
      ),
    ),
  )
}

## What the builder draws on

The builder draws the four parse passes — `LoomSourceRanges`, `WeftClassifier`, `WeftTokeniser`, and `LoomAstBuilder`, with `emptyDocument` and `emptyDocumentFor` for its recoveries — then `LoomDocumentBuilder` for the row of small passes, with the `Declared` it reads and `nothingDeclared`, and `ProductBuilder` for the product pass. From the token layer it takes `checkAnchorDelims` and the `InvalidAnchorDelims` errors it raises, which guard the configured delimiters before the parse, alongside `AnchorDelims` and `defaultAnchorDelims`. The `ParsedDocument`, `LoomModule`, and `Location` types name the tree `parse` gives, what a build returns, and how a loom is named. Effect supplies the composition primitives, `Data` for the tagged `ReadError` among them.

=>

import { Context, Data, Effect, Layer, Option, pipe } from 'effect'
import {
  checkAnchorDelims,
  defaultAnchorDelims,
  type AnchorDelims,
  type InvalidAnchorDelims,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import { LoomSourceRanges } from '#ast/LineRanges'
import { WeftClassifier } from '#ast/WeftClassifier'
import { WeftTokeniser } from '#ast/WeftTokeniser'
import { LoomAstBuilder, emptyDocument, emptyDocumentFor } from '#ast/LoomAstBuilder'
import type { ParsedDocument } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import { type Declared, LoomDocumentBuilder, nothingDeclared } from '#ast/LoomDocumentBuilder'
import { ProductBuilder } from '#ast/ProductBuilder'
import type { LoomModule, Location } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'

